# Cloudflare Workers static-asset headers.
# Copied to the output root by the `assets` entry in angular.json.

# E-44 (2026-08-20): security headers, applied to every path. The release-gating
# table (Private Roadmap.md) flags these as unreviewed - "nothing in this
# document set records these ever having been reviewed" - which was simply true
# until now.
#
# Four are enforcing immediately: they're well-understood and, critically,
# cannot break anything this app actually does, because none of them restrict
# WHERE the app is allowed to load scripts/styles/images/fetches FROM - they
# only harden things (HSTS, no-sniff, no-framing, referrer trimming) that no
# legitimate RangerTrak behaviour depends on.
#
# The Content-Security-Policy is NOT one of those four, and is deliberately
# Report-Only rather than enforcing. RangerTrak fetches from a real, non-trivial
# set of external origins at runtime - OpenStreetMap tiles, Nominatim geocoding,
# optionally Google's Geocoding API if a user configures a key in Settings,
# Google Fonts. An enforcing CSP that's missed one of those would SILENTLY
# break a real feature - most likely the offline map tiles or address
# geocoding, both of which a search-and-rescue exercise depends on -
# and there is no way to catch that from a local build: this repo's local static
# server does not read or apply `_headers` at all (that's a Cloudflare-only
# mechanism), so this policy is genuinely untested until it reaches the live
# edge. Report-Only logs violations to the browser console without blocking
# anything, which is the standard safe way to roll one out. Switching to
# enforcing needs someone actually using the deployed app for a while first and
# checking DevTools for violation reports - not a decision to make blind.
#
# No Strict-Transport-Security line here - confirmed live 2026-08-20 (curl
# against the deployed edge, twice, cache bypassed both times) that the zone
# already has Cloudflare's own dashboard-level HSTS enabled
# (SSL/TLS -> Edge Certificates -> HTTP Strict Transport Security), serving
# `max-age=15552000; includeSubDomains; preload` - which INCLUDES preload, a
# stronger commitment than this file was asking for, and which Cloudflare's
# zone-level feature serves regardless of what an origin header says. An origin
# HSTS declaration here would have been the exact "looks configured, does
# nothing" trap this project keeps finding (ADR D-39's catalogue) - it's not
# wrong, it's just never read. tools/check-deployed.js verifies the real
# zone-level value instead of a redundant origin declaration.
#
# Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy and Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy added 2026-08-26
# (E-44 audit, securityheaders.com pass) - both enforcing immediately, same
# "cannot break anything this app does" reasoning as the original four. COOP
# isolates this page's browsing context group from cross-origin openers/
# openees (no window.open() popups, no OAuth-style flows here). CORP:
# same-origin governs whether OTHER sites can embed RangerTrak's own JS/CSS/
# images - it has zero effect on RangerTrak's own outbound fetches to OSM/
# Nominatim/Fonts, and nothing legitimately needs to embed this app's assets
# cross-origin.
#
# Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy is deliberately Report-Only, NOT enforcing,
# same reasoning as the CSP above and for a concretely checked reason: COEP's
# strict `require-corp` mode would very likely break the map tiles outright -
# confirmed live 2026-08-26 that OpenStreetMap's tile servers and Nominatim
# send no `Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy` header, and Leaflet/MapLibre load
# tiles as plain no-cors `<img>`/tile requests, which `require-corp` blocks
# without one (their `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` doesn't help - that only
# matters for CORS-mode requests, not no-cors ones). `credentialless` mode
# was chosen instead of `require-corp` because it doesn't require a CORP
# header at all - it only strips credentials from cross-origin requests, and
# none of this app's cross-origin fetches (OSM tiles, Nominatim, Google
# Fonts, optionally Google Geocoding) ever send credentials to begin with -
# so enforcing it should be safe once observed, but "should be" is exactly
# why this is Report-Only first rather than assumed.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), camera=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy-Report-Only: credentialless
  Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com; img-src 'self' data: blob: https://*.tile.openstreetmap.org; connect-src 'self' https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org https://maps.googleapis.com https://api.what3words.com https://*.tile.openstreetmap.org; worker-src 'self'; manifest-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'

# Only the files that MUST NOT be cached are listed below. Everything else keeps
# Cloudflare's default ETag revalidation, which is already correct for the
# hash-named bundles (main-XXXX.js, chunk-XXXX.js, styles-XXXX.css) since a
# new build produces a new filename.
#
# These four are the service-worker control plane. If any of them is served
# stale, browsers keep running the previous release forever — the exact
# failure that masked the dead IONOS origin from existing users in 2026-08.

/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/ngsw.json
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/ngsw-worker.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/safety-worker.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/manifest.webmanifest
  Cache-Control: no-cache
